BEGIN_PGML
Remember that [`e`] is a real number (a constant). You may see it most often as part of an exponential function such as [`f(x)=e^x\text{,}`] but [`e`] itself is just a number. Which of the following statements about the number [`e`] are true, and which are false? Try to answer all of them *without using a calculator*.

a.  [`\newcommand{\lt}{<}2.5 \lt e \lt 3`] [_]{$popup1}{5}

a.  [`\newcommand{\lt}{<}14 \lt 7e \lt 21`] [_]{$popup2}{5}

a.  [`(e^2)^3 = e^5`]  [_]{$popup3}{5}

a.  [`\newcommand{\lt}{<}0 \lt e^3 \lt 27`]  [_]{$popup4}{5}

a.  [`e^a`] is positive for all real numbers [`a`]  [_]{$popup5}{5}

a.  [`e^{-1} = \frac{1}{e}`] [_]{$popup6}{5}

END_PGML

